{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Bereshit.</b> Why the Holy One, blessed be He, began the Torah with a bet and not with an alef, even though it is first of all the letters — to tell you: this bet is enclosed and surrounded on three sides — east, west, south — and open to the north. So too the Holy One, blessed be He, created the world enclosed from these three directions and open to the north, and from there evil and damaging winds come into the world. And so we find that the Name of the Holy One, blessed be He, is written with alef-dalet 134 times in Scripture, corresponding to the initial and final letters of the words for east, south, and west, which add up to this number, to say that they are locked in His Name — for ק״לד (134) is a term of locking, like קלידא (key). But the north is not included, until the Holy One, blessed be He, comes and locks it in His Name. And He left it open, to put idolaters on trial in the future, and He will say to them: let your fear come — if you can lock the north wind, which is open. For the other winds are locked in My Name. And when you count the entire alphabet you will find five hundred for the east and likewise for the west and likewise for the south, corresponding to the five hundred years' journey that there is across the breadth of each one." "<b>Bereshit.</b> Rashi said: Rabbi Yitzhak said, it was not necessary, etc. — because it is the first commandment with which Israel was commanded. And if you ask: are there not seven commandments, and the commandments of circumcision and the displaced sinew, which preceded the commandment of the new month as the first commandment? One may answer: the reason Rashi calls the commandment of the new moon the first commandment is because it is first among the commandments that Israel were commanded collectively, all of them together. Rashi explained why it begins with bereshit: because of ״כח מעשיו הגיד לעמו״ etc. And if you ask: how could the Canaanites say to Israel, it is stolen from the seven nations? First, this is not theft, for whatever a slave acquires, his master acquires, and it is established that Canaan was a slave to the descendants of Shem, as it is written, ״ויהי כנען עבד למו״. And furthermore, on the contrary, they stole it from Israel, as Rashi explained in the passage לך: ״והכנעני אז בארץ״ — he was going and conquering the land from the descendants of Shem, for in the portion of Shem it fell when Noah divided the land among his sons. Rather, one may answer: there were many kings in the land, such as the king of Hazor and his companions, who were not from Canaanite families, and likewise in the passage ברכה, concerning ״אפסי ארץ״, and perhaps they were of the descendants of Shem, and Israel had no authority over them to take it from them by force, and from them the Canaanite was going and conquering, as Rashi explained. Another interpretation: the reason it begins with bereshit is to make known the order of Creation and the giving of the land, as Rashi explained — that is the reason, so that the Canaanites have no opening to say to Israel: you are not the rightful heir to inherit, for he is Elam, but you are from Arpachshad, the youngest of the sons, as it is written, ״בני שם עילם ואשור וארפכשד״ — if so, you conquered the land unlawfully. Another interpretation: for this reason it began with the matter of Creation, for otherwise they would say: the minor of faith, the world proceeds according to its custom and has always stood thus. Rashi: ״ברצונו נתנה להם״ etc. — it should read, He did not give it to them permanently, for if so, how could He retract His gift. Rashi explained: there is no reishit in Scripture that is not attached to the word after it. But we have found a parallel: ״כי אחרית לאיש שלום״ — and it is not attached to the word after it." "<b>Bereshit bara.</b> At the very first the Creator created <b>et the heavens and et the earth</b>, like ״בראשית ממלכות יהויקים״, and so Targum: ״בקדמין ברא״. <b>Elohim.</b> Etc. If there be a person in whose heart it arises to find wonder in the word and language of אלקי, אלקים, אלקיך, אלקינו and many like them that are expressed in plural language — how are they written concerning the Holy One, blessed be He, who is One? — such as ״אלקי אברהם יצחק ויעקב״ and many more — let that person consider and reflect upon his heart, lest there be in him a root bearing gall and wormwood. For words of divinity, lordship, and mastery are written for a singular in plural language, such as ״כי שם נגלו אליו האלקים״; ״דבר האיש אדוני הארץ״; ״אם בעליו עמו״. And do not answer me concerning the holiness of the word Adonai — that it is called in plural language in reference to the Holy One, blessed be He, who is One — for it is not of their derivation but rather of the pattern of Shaddai, זכאי, בנאי, כשדאי, ארמאי, דוי, אשמאי. And it is true that divinity is expressed in plural language also in reference to the Holy One, blessed be He, who is One and unique, for the word divinity is a term of dominion, and so one of them explained, and from it the others are clarified and examined and sought out from their hidden meanings: ״אנכי ה׳ אלקיך״ — I am all your words of lordship, sovereignty, and dominion; not so is the measure of flesh and blood, who does not rule over all the members of his kingdom all the days of their lives, for he dies and his son comes after him, or another arises in his stead, or they are ruled by a different sovereignty. Behold, after these true matters it is known that there are many human beings over whom, in their lifetimes, there are several kinds of lordships and authorities from several persons, one after another, for masters of flesh and blood die and perish, while the Holy One, blessed be He, was king and is king and will be king, and His kingdom rules over all, and in His hand to exalt and to give strength to all." "<b>Et the heavens.</b> Rabbi Yishmael asked Rabbi Akiva: this <b>את</b> written here — ״את השמים ואת הארץ״ — what is it? He said to him: had it said 'Elohim created השמים', I might have said that 'heavens' and 'earth' are divinities; now that it writes <b>et the heavens and et the earth</b>, it is known that the heavens and the earth are the created ones." "<b>The heavens</b> — the matter written concerns the specified created things known and visible to us. <b>שמים</b> is plural and does not have a singular, like מים, חיים, פנים, מלקחיים, רחיים. And ordinary <b>שמים</b> is a term of height; <b>וארץ</b>, a term of lowness and what is below, like ״בהנשא החיות מעל הארץ״, meaning: above the lower firmament."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {4 "The phrase 'ולא יתפרדו' is understood here as 'does not have a singular form' (lit. 'is not separated'), following the examples of plural-only nouns that follow."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:18:12.089118090Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}